// EXPECT: E0277 — a `has_note` note name that is not a string literal (ADR-0099 §1)
//
// **A `has_note` note name must be a string literal** (E0277, ADR-0099 §1).
//
// `has_note(f, "x")` is folded while *checking* — the answer is in the HIR's `Proc::notes`, which the checker
// is already holding, which is what makes the reader need no VM and no query at all. So the name has to be
// readable then, and a computed one is not: const-eval runs downstream of sema (ADR-0018 §3).
//
// This is the same narrowing an array length took (ADR-0039 §3a) and an `#insert` operand took before it
// (ADR-0072), and it has the same widening route available later (ADR-0070 §1) if a caller ever wants a
// computed note name. Refused rather than silently answering `false`, because a `false` from a name the
// compiler could not read is indistinguishable from a note that is genuinely absent — and telling those two
// apart is the entire job of a reader.
// No `#import "Basic"`, because nothing here needs it and an unused import is E0231 — a *second*
// diagnostic, which this directory's one-per-file contract does not allow. `has_note` is recognised by the
// compiler rather than imported, so the refusal does not depend on a module being loaded.

tuned :: (x: s64) -> s64 @fast {
    return x;
}

asks_with_a_local :: () {
    which := "fast";
    // A local, so the name resolves — the refusal is about *when* it is readable, not whether it exists.
    answer := has_note(tuned, which);
}
